      "text": "Cyprian's mean score is the sum of the scores divided by the number of scores. So, if Cyprian's mean score is 90, we know that the total sum of his scores is 90 multiplied by 3, or 270. We can subtract out one score from this total to find the sum of the other two scores:\n\\begin{align*}\n270 - 85 &= 185 \\\\\n270 - 87 &= 183 \\\\\n270 - 92 &= 178 \\\\\n270 - 93 &= 177 \\\\\n270 - 94 &= 176 \\\\\n270 - 98 &= 172\n\\end{align*}\nSince Cyprian's mean score is 90, and he scored one of each of these possible sums, his scores are:\n$$\\left(270 - 90\\right), \\left(270 - 90\\right), and \\left(270 - 90\\right).$$\nTherefore, Margaret's mean score must also be 90.\n\nOther test simulation problems by Rachel:\nIf one irrational number added to another __\nWhich of $\\pi$, $\\pi^2$ and $e^e$ is the greatest?\nWhich of $\\sqrt2$, $\\sqrt3$ and $\\sqrt5$ is the greatest?\nWhich of $\\sqrt3$ and $\\sqrt5$ is greater?\n\n70\nwww.mathcounts.org/sectionalstuff/pastrel/categoriesByDate/?id=2017RC\n\n$87, 94$ and $98$\n\nThe difference between an irrational number and a rational is an irrational number. So $\\sqrt 2 - \\sqrt 3$ must be greater than zero and less than one which means $\\sqrt2>\\sqrt3$\n\nIt is equal to 60 so C\n\nI can't find any irrational number greater than $\\pi^2$\n\nEDIT: you can actually find a number that is closer to it.  But it is still $9.8696$\n\nWhoever gave me an inaccuracy check question is my friend at RC counting to 1.\n\nIf we have $a\\neq 0$,$b\\neq 0$,$ab\\neq 0$\nThen $a^b=e^{\\ln(a^b)}=e^\\frac{\\ln(a^2)}{a^2}\\cdot e^\\frac{\\ln(b^2)}{b^2}=...$.  So if all our logarithms are equally far away from 0, our answer will be the same as that in the question.  But how big can the logarithms get?  They can get infinitely big aka undefined, right?  But we have $e$ in the range of fractions (4 -> 7 on a calculator).  At this point, I think we can see how this all turns and instead of getting \"out of cycle\" solutions of $112, 140, 160, 186, 200$, we are getting nice, finite, equal solutions $70, 80, 85, 90, 95$.  How does this help?\n\ndivvy this up among the school board, reporters, board of directors, but by far grade cops\n\nThe relevant last step of the solution above is to take everything out to an $(\\log a)^2$ and $(\\log b)^2$ and then realize that we don't know the absolute values of $\\log a$ and $\\log b$ as well.  I remember hearing 240 but that's not the end of the story.\n\nYes the only way to deference words like graduating is to make them a grade, but I'd rather have a 2 on something than be in an embarrassed completely careless Academics classroom that still can't see the obvious points on busy work.\n\nthis is an annoying fake ingenious trap, $\\neq$ is  a thing which truncate the eventual answer and you need to ban it as well\n\namg Award for this\nBe like  $241.51241$ or something like that\n\nbetting recreant use \"don't\" instead of \"wouldn't\"\n\"And mot elite Arcadi...\"\n\nThey mean $\\neq$, which is a thing in set theory. If no, then I would totally trust the setters\nMake it a 29 (not 39)\n\nOk, now I seriously cannot look at all the 31+ answers \n\nthis is abuse of absurdity gripped by a synthetic darkness\nmake it $180.4869....$\n\n[B] Archambe][B]\nProof:\na!=0, b!=0, ab!=0\n$\\log ne^{\\log a^2a^2}= \\log ne^{2\\log a}= 2\\log n+\\log e^{\\log a}= 2\\log n+\\log ae^{\\log a}$\nand likewise for b\n$ne^{\\log b^2}= \\log ne^{2\\log b}= 2\\log n+\\log e^{2\\log b}= 2\\log n+2\\log b\\cdot e^{\\log b}$\nAnd a!b= $ab\\cdot\\frac{e^{\\log a}e^{\\log b}}{e^{\\log a}e^{\\log b}}$\n\nSlitherfish, childish abuse of language does no harm. Perfect correctable mistake.\n\nWhat might help start younger thinking?\n\nI think learning elementary logic is nonnegative, and teaching elementary logic walks someone down a path intoducing them to logic through informal methods at the outset. There are a lot of math related sub areas suitable places to guide the young mind with some underst